    "facts": [
      "Built 1933-34 for storage of perishable goods (cold store) in the Nieuwe Bouwen style; architects J. de Bie Leuveling Tjeenk and K. Bakker.",
      "A bridge over the IJhaven was planned straight through the warehouse; the Jan Schaeferbrug (opened 2001) was built with the monument kept. Rijksmonument since 2001, restored by Stadsherstel Amsterdam, in use again September 2006 (reuse project André van Stigt)."
    ]
  },
  "measuredBy": {
    "3dbag": "NL.IMBAG.Pand.0363100012088034 LoD2.2: ground 0.52 m NAP, roofMax 27.99 m NAP; ring 35.5 x 28.8 m rotated 16 degrees. 3DBAG fuses the overhang into solid walls to the ground, and carries a 0.5 m chimney stack to 34.4 m (capped at roofMax).",
    "faces": "south 196 deg (35.5 m): four rows of 0.76 m windows in blocks of 3 / 3 / 4 columns, two orange-shuttered stair towers (3DBAG tower walls at u 11.0-15.0 and 22.25-25.25 m from the west end), DE ZWYGER lettering at the east end; north 16 deg (34.4 m): five tall glazed strips (narrow, wide, narrow, wide, narrow), converted 2006; east 106 deg (28.8 m): blank brick with six staggered pale lettering bands; west 286 deg: not photographed.",
    "portal": "Roadway of the Jan Schaeferbrug passes through the ground floor on the OSM road line, centred 11.4 m from the west end of both the north and south faces (panoramas run along it at x -3..1 m, aerial confirms), 13 m wide, soffit about 6.0 m (estimated 5.5-6.5).",
    "scale": "Window pitch calibrated on the south face with the tower walls of the 3DBAG shell (tower centres 13.0 and 23.75 m from the west end matched the 2005 photo to 0.3 m)."
  },
